# KNOWN GAP (todo/t0977) — `auto out = v.map(<closure>)` where the closure's
# RESULT type differs from the source element type and is a heap `String`
# reads back a RAW POINTER. `gg check` is clean, the element COUNT is right,
# and only the values are garbage — the failure mode a length assertion sleeps
# through.
#
# THE AXIS IS THREE-DIMENSIONAL and no single column is the defect. Measured
# 2026-09-03, `gg run`, one program:
#
#   | # | destination        | callee          | source -> result | HEAD      |
#   |---|--------------------|-----------------|------------------|-----------|
#   | 1 | `auto`             | closure, untyped| User -> String   | **GARBAGE** |
#   | 2 | `auto`             | closure, untyped| User -> int      | correct   |
#   | 3 | `Vector[String]`   | closure, untyped| User -> String   | correct   |
#   | 4 | `auto`             | NAMED function  | User -> String   | correct   |
#   | 5 | `auto`             | closure, TYPED  | User -> String   | **GARBAGE** |
#   | 6 | `auto`             | closure, untyped| String -> String | correct   |
#
# Cells 2/3/4/6 are LOAD-BEARING, not filler. 3 says the destination is half
# of it (a declared element type repairs the same expression). 4 says the
# CALLEE KIND is the other half (a named function with the identical signature
# is correct, so this is not the protocol's return-type inference in general).
# 6 says it takes a CROSS-TYPE map — a String->String map through the same
# closure path is correct — and 2 says the result element must be a HEAP type.
# The defect is the conjunction: auto destination AND closure callee AND
# cross-type AND heap result.
#
# HEAP-FORCED: every `String` here is built at runtime by `mk`, never a static
# literal, so the value read back is a real heap payload and a pointer printed
# in its place is unmistakable.
#
# SAME SYMPTOM AS `todo/t0823` γ1a — *"the Rust half was the builtin protocol's
# `ret_self` (`auto` destination wrong, typed destination right)"* — which R48
# Track γ closed for the SAME-TYPE case (`nums.map((int n): …)`). This is the
# CROSS-TYPE cell of that class, still live. NOT `todo/t0954`: that is a LEAK
# (the accumulator's missing `elem_drop`) on a program whose stdout is CORRECT;
# here stdout is WRONG and the leak is beside the point.
#
# INTENDED (asserted by the #[ignore]d wired test): a / 20 / a / a / a / ab!

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

struct User:
    String name
    int age

String tag(User u):
    return u.name

void main():
    Vector[User] users = [User(mk("a", ""), 20), User(mk("b", ""), 30)]

    auto c1 = users.map((u): u.name)
    print(c1.get(0).unwrap())

    auto c2 = users.map((u): u.age)
    print(c2.get(0).unwrap())

    Vector[String] c3 = users.map((u): u.name)
    print(c3.get(0).unwrap())

    auto c4 = users.map(tag)
    print(c4.get(0).unwrap())

    auto c5 = users.map((User u): u.name)
    print(c5.get(0).unwrap())

    Vector[String] words = [mk("a", "b"), mk("c", "d")]
    auto c6 = words.map((w): w + "!")
    print(c6.get(0).unwrap())
